==============================================================================================
Boundary Condition-based Machine Learning Algorithm for Fast Prediction of Supersonic Flows
==============================================================================================

Rachakonda Naga Sai Prakash (రాచకొండ నాగ సాయి ప్రకాష్), Tapan K. Mankodi(तपन के. मंकोड़ी), Niranjan Sahoo (ନରଞ୍ଜନ ସାହ)
Department of Mechanical Engineering, Indian Institute of Technology Guwahati, Guwahati, Assam, 781039, India
(*Electronic mail: tapan.mankodi@iitg.ac.in)
(Dated: June 10, 2025)


The supplementary material contains
1) model16GenObjC3012a.keras
	KERAS API for $S_{16}$ BCML Model 
	This is the main DNN model that the python codes will use
2) Case folders:
	a) 0 						Initial Conditions
	b) 0.1 						NSF Results for comparison
	c) constant 				Mesh and other details
	d) system					solver and domain decomposition
3) InputFileWith16Generators_Validation.txt
	Each row contains 9 times 16 = 144 values for a cell
	Total number of cell (check constant/polyMesh/) is same as the number of lines in this file
	Columns 9i+1 contains the length of the generator (where i in (0, 1, 2, ... , 8)), not the distance function
4) Python files
	a) ML_TrainBCML.py 			for training the BCML model
	b) ML_LoadBCML.py 			for predicting flow field using model16GenObjC3012a.keras BCML model


p, T, U files should be in "0" Folder
p1, Ttrans1, U1 should be in "0.1" Folder.
fvschemes, fvsolution, controlDict and  decomposeParDict  : all 4 files should be in " System Folder"





To run the model
1) Run $python3 ML_LoadBCML.py
2) Make a new folder (say 0.2) and copy paste p, T and U files into this folder
3) Use Paraview for visualization.
